WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.882
The same table can support several aggregate measures in one query.

00:00:03.882 --> 00:00:07.652
This example summarizes sales using functions such as SUM, AVG, MIN, and MAX.

00:00:07.652 --> 00:00:10.262
Before running it, make a prediction.

00:00:10.262 --> 00:00:16.362
The total quantity and total revenue answer different questions, while the minimum and maximum show the range

00:00:16.362 --> 00:00:22.772
of individual values. Run the query in the browser and inspect each result column.

00:00:22.772 --> 00:00:27.717
Notice that PostgreSQL can calculate all of these measures from the same set of source rows.

00:00:27.717 --> 00:00:32.122
The important idea is not to add every aggregate function just because you can.

00:00:32.122 --> 00:00:37.742
Choose measures that support the analytical question.

00:00:37.742 --> 00:00:43.072
A decision-maker usually needs a small set of clearly named metrics rather than every possible summary.

00:00:43.072 --> 00:00:47.922
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:47.922 --> 00:00:52.077
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:52.077 --> 00:00:54.682
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:54.682 --> 00:01:00.362
In a real setting such as a retail report comparing revenue across products and regions,

00:01:00.362 --> 00:01:06.587
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:01:06.587 --> 00:01:08.857
before discussing syntax.

00:01:08.857 --> 00:01:15.038
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
